<script module>
  import Demo from "$ui/components/Demo.abide"
  import MemoDerivedDemo from "$ui/demos/memo/MemoDerivedDemo.abide"
  import MemoSourceDemo from "$ui/demos/memo/MemoSourceDemo.abide"
  import MemoSourcesDemo from "$ui/demos/memo/MemoSourcesDemo.abide"
  import MemoProjectionDemo from "$ui/demos/memo/MemoProjectionDemo.abide"
  import MemoKeyedSyncDemo from "$ui/demos/memo/MemoKeyedSyncDemo.abide"
  import MemoAsyncDemo from "$ui/demos/memo/MemoAsyncDemo.abide"
  import MemoArgsDemo from "$ui/demos/memo/MemoArgsDemo.abide"
  import ServerStateDemo from "$ui/demos/memo/ServerStateDemo.abide"
</script>

<main class="page">
  <h1><code>memo</code> — the loaded value</h1>
  <p class="lead">The second primitive: one memoizer covering everything derived. <strong>A memo's dependencies are its declared inputs</strong> (ADR 0024) — declare args and they are the cache key; declare none and they are inferred from what the body reads. That single rule spans a synchronous derivation off a <a href="/state"><code>state</code></a> and an async slot with cache, coalescing, and a reactive read surface. It is why there is no separate <code>computed</code> or <code>linked</code> primitive, and why <a href="/rpc"><code>rpc = memo + transport</code></a>.</p>
  <Demo id="derived" title="<code>memo(() =&gt; …)</code> — auto-tracked derivation" client="src/ui/demos/memo/MemoDerivedDemo.abide">
    <MemoDerivedDemo />
  </Demo>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p>An argless, <em>synchronous</em> body is auto-tracked: it subscribes to whatever it reads and recomputes lazily. Its bare call returns <code>T</code>, not a promise, so it renders in the initial HTML instead of blanking and refilling a microtask later. Drive <code>count</code> and <code>doubled</code> follows, updating only its text node.</p>
  </aside>
  <Demo id="source" title="<code>memo(source, transform)</code> — the source is the dependency" client="src/ui/demos/memo/MemoSourceDemo.abide">
    <MemoSourceDemo />
  </Demo>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p>Pass a thunk and what it reads becomes the whole dependency set: <code>memo(source, transform)</code> tracks the thunk only, and the transform runs untracked — so a transform can read anything without that read becoming a trigger. Here it reads <code>factor</code>, so bumping factor alone recomputes nothing; the next change to <code>count</code> picks up its current value.</p>
    <p>The source is <em>always</em> a thunk (ADR 0025), which is why there is no dependency-declaring syntax to learn: the tracked region is an arrow body you can point at. Same rule as <code>watch(source, handler)</code>.</p>
  </aside>
  <Demo id="sources" title="<code>memo(() =&gt; ({'{'} a, b {'}'}), transform)</code> — several declared inputs" client="src/ui/demos/memo/MemoSourcesDemo.abide">
    <MemoSourcesDemo />
  </Demo>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p>Several inputs need no API of their own — they are what the thunk returns. Every read inside it is tracked, and the transform still receives ONE argument. Return an object and they stay named, so adding an input never shifts the others.</p>
  </aside>
  <Demo id="projection" title="<code>memo(…).state()</code> — writable projection" client="src/ui/demos/memo/MemoProjectionDemo.abide">
    <MemoProjectionDemo />
  </Demo>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p>A memo derives, it doesn't hold — <code>.state()</code> projects one back into a writable cell whose <code>set</code> IS <code>publish</code>, so a local write is provisional and holds only until the next re-fill. That is the shape behind every editable field seeded from loaded data: the draft seeds to <code>count × 100</code>, bumping it edits locally, and changing <code>count</code> reseeds and discards those edits.</p>
  </aside>
  <Demo id="keyed-sync" title="<code>memo((args) =&gt; …)</code> — the argument is the cache key" client="src/ui/demos/memo/MemoKeyedSyncDemo.abide">
    <MemoKeyedSyncDemo />
  </Demo>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p>Declare an argument and it becomes the cache key, so the memo keeps one slot per key — <code>{'{'} w: 3, h: 4 {'}'}</code> and <code>{'{'} w: 2, h: 5 {'}'}</code> are independent, each reused on the next read with the same key.</p>
    <p>The args are then the whole dependency set, so the body runs untracked. A synchronous body stays synchronous: the read is the value, not a promise, so it renders straight into the server HTML.</p>
  </aside>
  <Demo id="memo-async" title="<code>memo(async () =&gt; …)</code> — one loaded slot" client="src/ui/demos/memo/MemoAsyncDemo.abide" tab="client">
    <MemoAsyncDemo />
  </Demo>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p>The other axis — an async body. No args again, so it is one slot, but a <em>loaded</em> one: the read is coalesced (concurrent callers share one run), the settled value is retained, and the slot carries the full read surface (<code>live</code>/<code>pending</code>/<code>refreshing</code>/<code>error</code>/<code>watch</code> + <code>refresh</code>/<code>invalidate</code>/<code>publish</code>).</p>
    <p>What that costs: an argless async body is NOT auto-tracked. Half-tracked is worse than untracked, so a load re-fills on <code>refresh</code>/<code>invalidate</code> only. Read again and the run count holds, rename and nothing re-runs, <code>refresh</code> re-runs and picks up the current label.</p>
  </aside>
  <Demo id="memo-args" title="<code>memo(async (args) =&gt; …)</code> — keyed AND loaded" client="src/ui/demos/memo/MemoArgsDemo.abide" tab="client">
    <MemoArgsDemo />
  </Demo>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p>Both axes at once, and nothing new to learn: the async body of the card above, keyed like the card before it. One loaded slot becomes one loaded slot per key — <code>alpha</code> and <code>beta</code> load independently, and <code>invalidate({'{'} key: "alpha" {'}'})</code> drops that slot alone. This is exactly the shape an RPC takes: <code>rpc = memo + transport</code>, and the transport is the only difference.</p>
  </aside>
  <Demo id="server-state" title="<code>state</code> + <code>memo</code> + <code>watch</code> across server modules" server="src/shared/serverReactive.ts" client="src/ui/demos/memo/ServerStateDemo.abide" tab="server">
    <ServerStateDemo />
  </Demo>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p><code>state</code> and <code>memo</code> live in <code>abide/shared/*</code>, so neither is client-only. A server module can own a <code>state</code> while other modules import it, derive from it (<code>memo</code>) and subscribe to it (<code>watch</code>) — a reactive graph running entirely on the server. Here two RPCs write and read one cell in <code>$shared/serverReactive</code>.</p>
    <p>The server tab spells out <code>total()</code> and <code>total.set(x)</code>: there is no compiler in a plain <code>.ts</code>, so you call the <a href="/state"><code>State</code></a> surface directly. Same cell either way — the bare-identifier form is <code>.abide</code> sugar.</p>
    <p class="note">Module-level server <code>state</code> is process-global (one value across all requests) — right for a global like this counter; for mutable per-request or per-user state reach for <code>memo: {'{'} shared {'}'}</code>, which is guarded and identity-safe.</p>
  </aside>
</main>
